.Dd August 7, 2019
.Dt PTHREAD_MUTEX_TIMEDLOCK 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pthread_mutex_clocklock ,
.Nm pthread_mutex_timedlock
.Nd lock a mutex without blocking indefinitely
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libpthread
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In pthread.h
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fo pthread_mutex_clocklock
.Fa "pthread_mutex_t *restrict mutex"
.Fa "clockid_t clockid"
.Fa "const struct timespec *restrict abs_timeout"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo pthread_mutex_timedlock
.Fa "pthread_mutex_t *restrict mutex"
.Fa "const struct timespec *restrict abs_timeout"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
function locks
.Fa mutex .
If the mutex is already locked, the calling thread blocks until the
mutex becomes available or the timeout expires.
The timeout expires when the clock specified by
.Fa clockid
reaches or passes the absolute time
.Fa abs_timeout ;
it is not relative to the current time.
If the mutex can be locked immediately, the call does not fail
with a timeout, and
.Fa abs_timeout
and
.Fa clockid
are not checked for validity.
.Pp
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_timedlock
function is equivalent to
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
with
.Fa clockid
set to
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
If successful,
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
and
.Fn pthread_mutex_timedlock
will return zero, otherwise an error number will be returned to
indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
and
.Fn pthread_mutex_timedlock
functions will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOTRECOVERABLE
The
.Fa mutex
was created with the protocol attribute having the
value PTHREAD_PRIO_PROTECT and
the calling thread's priority is higher than the
mutex's current priority ceiling.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The process or thread would have blocked, and
.Fa abs_timeout
specified a nanosecond value less than zero or
greater than or equal to 1 billion.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa mutex
parameter is invalid.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa clockid
parameter is invalid.
The
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
and
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
clocks are always supported; see
.Xr _umtx_op 2
for the full list of accepted clocks.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The
.Fa mutex
could not be locked before the timeout expired.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The
.Fa mutex
could not be acquired because the
maximum number of recursive locks for the
.Fa mutex
has been exceeded.
.It Bq Er EDEADLK
The current thread already owns the error-checking
.Fa mutex .
.It Bq Er EOWNERDEAD
The argument
.Fa mutex
points to a robust mutex and the process containing the previous owning
thread terminated while holding the mutex lock.
The lock was granted to the caller and it is up to the new owner
to make the state consistent.
.It Bq Er ENOTRECOVERABLE
The state protected by the
.Fa mutex
is not recoverable.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pthread_mutex_consistent 3 ,
.Xr pthread_mutex_destroy 3 ,
.Xr pthread_mutex_init 3 ,
.Xr pthread_mutex_lock 3 ,
.Xr pthread_mutex_trylock 3 ,
.Xr pthread_mutex_unlock 3
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_timedlock
function conforms to
.St -p1003.1-96 .
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
function conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2024 .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn pthread_mutex_clocklock
function first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
